  "title": "The 2026 PAU exam: structure",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>The History of Philosophy exam assesses <strong>intellectual maturity</strong>, analysis and critical reasoning, and your understanding of the main problems and concepts of the subject. Here is the map of the exam; each exercise has its own guide in this section (and the essay, in the <strong>Essays</strong> tab).</p> </blockquote>\n\n<h2>General guidelines</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>There is <strong>only one paper</strong> (you do not choose between two models).</li>\n<li>It may include questions with <strong>two options (A or B)</strong>.</li>\n<li>That optionality <strong>does not guarantee</strong> that you can get full marks without having prepared some of the blocks of basic knowledge.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>The three exercises</h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Exercise</th><th>What it asks for</th><th>Marks</th><th>Competences</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>1 · Text comprehension</strong></td><td>Summary (1.1) + comparison/explanation on the text (1.2, A or B). Text of 200–400 words.</td><td><strong>4</strong></td><td>CE1, CE7</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>2 · Philosophical reflection</strong></td><td>An argued <strong>essay</strong> (for/against + opinion). Single option, 200–300 words.</td><td><strong>3</strong></td><td>CE2, CE3, CE8, CE9</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>3 · Rethinking the HoP</strong></td><td>Argued exposition of a question from the History of Philosophy. Option A or B, 200–300 words.</td><td><strong>3</strong></td><td>CE4, CE5, CE6</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Spelling</strong>: each mistake deducts <strong>−0.1</strong>, up to a maximum of −1 mark.</p>\n\n<h2>Indicative texts 2025–26 (Exercise 1)</h2>\n<p>For each exam session, between 4 and 8 groups of texts are selected; they are indicative, not fixed. Those proposed this year —all available in the <strong>Readings</strong> tab—:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Plato</strong>: <em>The Republic</em>, Book VII.</li>\n<li><strong>Descartes</strong>: <em>Discourse on Method</em>, parts II, III and IV.</li>\n<li><strong>Kant</strong>: <em>An Answer to the Question: What Is Enlightenment?</em></li>\n<li><strong>Nietzsche</strong>: <em>Ecce Homo</em>, ‘Why I Am a Destiny’.</li>\n<li><strong>Beauvoir</strong>: <em>The Second Sex</em>, Conclusion.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>And Exercise 2?</h2>\n<p>Exercise 2 is the <strong>philosophical essay</strong>. You will find its method, the rubric and several worked models in the <strong>Essays</strong> tab.</p>\n\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n</blockquote>"
