  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>Where each text and each quotation that appears in these guides is to be found.</p> </blockquote>\n\n<h2>The five readings</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Plato</strong>, <em>Republic</em>, Book VII, 514a-541b (Stephanus pagination). Fragments: 517b-c and 519e-520a. Translation quoted in these guides: Patricio de Azcárate, in <em>Obras completas de Platón</em>, Madrid, Medina y Navarro, 1872, vols. 7–8 (public domain). Translation in the Readings tab: José Manuel Pabón and Manuel Fernández-Galiano, Madrid, Instituto de Estudios Políticos, 1949 (reprinted by Alianza).</li>\n<li><strong>Descartes, R.</strong>, <em>Discours de la méthode</em>, Leiden, Jan Maire, 1637. Reference edition: <em>Œuvres de Descartes</em>, ed. Ch. Adam and P. Tannery, vol. VI (AT VI): part II, 11-22; part III, 22-31; part IV, 31-40. Translation: Manuel García Morente, <em>Discurso del método. Meditaciones metafísicas</em>, Madrid, Espasa-Calpe, Austral series.</li>\n<li><strong>Kant, I.</strong>, ‘Beantwortung der Frage: Was ist Aufklärung?’, <em>Berlinische Monatsschrift</em>, December 1784, pp. 481-494. Academy edition: Ak. VIII, 33-42. Translations: Eugenio Ímaz, in <em>Filosofía de la historia</em>, Mexico, FCE, 1941; Agapito Maestre and José Romagosa, in <em>¿Qué es Ilustración?</em>, Madrid, Tecnos, 1988.</li>\n<li><strong>Nietzsche, F.</strong>, <em>Ecce homo. Wie man wird, was man ist</em>, written in 1888 and published in 1908; ‘Warum ich ein Schicksal bin’, §§ 1-9. Critical edition: <em>Kritische Studienausgabe</em> (KSA), ed. Colli and Montinari, vol. 6, pp. 365-374. Translation: Andrés Sánchez Pascual, Madrid, Alianza, 1971.</li>\n<li><strong>Beauvoir, S. de</strong>, <em>Le deuxième sexe</em>, Paris, Gallimard, 1949, vol. II, ‘Conclusion’. Translation: Alicia Martorell, <em>El segundo sexo</em>, Madrid, Cátedra, Feminismos series, 1998-1999 (in one volume, 2005). In Basque: <em>Bigarren sexua</em>, trans. Irene Arrarats, Elkar-Jakin, 2019.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Quotations and references used in the model answers</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Protagoras</strong>, ‘man is the measure of all things’: fragment DK 80 B 1; transmitted by Plato, <em>Theaetetus</em>, 152a.</li>\n<li><strong>Socrates</strong>, ‘no one does evil willingly’: Plato, <em>Protagoras</em>, 345d-e and 358c-d.</li>\n<li><strong>Plato</strong>, rhetoric as cookery as against medicine: <em>Gorgias</em>, 464b-465e. The simile of the sun: <em>Republic</em> VI, 508a-509b.</li>\n<li><strong>Aristotle</strong>, <em>akrasia</em>: <em>Nicomachean Ethics</em>, VII, 1-10 (1145a-1152a).</li>\n<li><strong>Cicero</strong>, the Stoic archer: <em>De finibus</em>, III, 22; duty and right action (<em>kathêkon</em> and <em>katórthōma</em>): III, 58-59.</li>\n<li><strong>Aspasia</strong>: Plato, <em>Menexenus</em>, 235e-236c; Xenophon, <em>Memorabilia</em>, II, 6, 36, and <em>Oeconomicus</em>, III, 14; Aristophanes, <em>Acharnians</em>, 524-529.</li>\n<li><strong>Hypatia</strong>: Socrates Scholasticus, <em>Ecclesiastical History</em>, VII, 15; <em>Suda</em>, s. v. ‘Hypatia’.</li>\n<li><strong>Hildegard of Bingen</strong>, <em>Scivias</em> (1141-1151).</li>\n<li><strong>Tertullian</strong>, ‘what has Athens to do with Jerusalem?’: <em>De praescriptione haereticorum</em>, 7, 9.</li>\n<li><strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong>, ‘crede ut intelligas’: <em>Sermon</em> 43, 7, 9; also <em>Tractates on the Gospel of John</em>, 29, 6.</li>\n<li><strong>Anselm of Canterbury</strong>, ‘credo ut intelligam’: <em>Proslogion</em>, ch. 1.</li>\n<li><strong>Thomas Aquinas</strong>, the five ways: <em>Summa Theologiae</em>, I, q. 2, a. 3; the preambles of faith: I, q. 2, a. 2, ad 1; the two orders of truths: <em>Summa contra Gentiles</em>, I, 3-8. The maxim ‘nihil est in intellectu quod non prius fuerit in sensu’: <em>De veritate</em>, q. 2, a. 3, arg. 19.</li>\n<li><strong>Locke, J.</strong>, the mind as a blank sheet: <em>An Essay concerning Human Understanding</em> (1690), II, i, 2.</li>\n<li><strong>Hume, D.</strong>, the self as a bundle of perceptions: <em>A Treatise of Human Nature</em> (1739-1740), I, iv, 6; the origin of ideas in impressions: I, i, 1, and <em>An Enquiry concerning Human Understanding</em> (1748), sec. 2.</li>\n<li><strong>Kant, I.</strong>, ‘thoughts without content are empty; intuitions without concepts are blind’: <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em>, A 51 / B 75. The critique of the self as substance: ‘Paralogisms of Pure Reason’, A 341 / B 399 ff. The ‘dogmatic slumber’: <em>Prolegomena</em> (1783), Ak. IV, 260.</li>\n<li><strong>Nietzsche, F.</strong>, ‘a thought comes when “it” wants, not when “I” want’: <em>Beyond Good and Evil</em> (1886), § 17. ‘There are no moral phenomena, only a moral interpretation of phenomena’: § 108. ‘There are no facts, only interpretations’: posthumous fragment 7[60], 1886-1887 (KSA 12, p. 315).</li>\n<li><strong>Gouges, O. de</strong>, <em>Déclaration des droits de la femme et de la citoyenne</em>, Paris, 1791.</li>\n<li><strong>Wollstonecraft, M.</strong>, <em>A Vindication of the Rights of Woman</em>, London, J. Johnson, 1792.</li>\n<li><strong>Beauvoir, S. de</strong>, ‘on ne naît pas femme : on le devient’: <em>Le deuxième sexe</em>, vol. II, part one, ch. I.</li>\n<li><strong>Marx, K.</strong>, man’s relation to woman as the measure of humanisation (the quotation with which Beauvoir closes the ‘Conclusion’): <em>Economic and Philosophic Manuscripts of 1844</em>, third manuscript, ‘Private Property and Communism’.</li>\n</ul>"
